  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{4t}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{\u2033}-3{y}^{\u2032}-4y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(t)={e}^{-t-1}$\n- $y(t)=7{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-4t}$\n- $y(t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{4}$",
  "worked_solution": "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{4t}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(t)={e}^{-t-1}=\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{e}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{e},{c}_{2}=0.$\n\n- The function $y(t)=7{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-4t}$ is **not** a solution of the given equation because ${e}^{-4t}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{4}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=0,{c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$",
